{"id":7778751884971,"type":"song","name":"Doctor My Eyes","artist":"Jackson Browne","year":1972,"weight":11,"info":{"cover":"https://cdn-images.dzcdn.net/images/cover/7dd26423779eb41b1f00d4aee3de0ea0/250x250-000000-80-0-0.jpg","album":"The Very Best Of Jackson Browne","yt":"gtlqZOT2fOo"},"artist_ref":{"id":109271562000552,"name":"Jackson Browne","info":{"img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Jackson_Browne_1977.JPG?width=500"}},"labels":[],"genres":[],"lists":[{"id":68619710521645,"name":"Billboard Year-End 1972","src":"singles","grp":"billboard_year_end","list_year":1972,"rank":92,"year":1972,"note":"","total":100,"scaruffi":null},{"id":132335824845498,"name":"Billboard Year-End Hot 100","src":"charts","grp":"chart","list_year":null,"rank":92,"year":1972,"note":null,"total":6841,"scaruffi":null}],"podcast":[{"kind":"podcast","title":null,"body":"Recorded in 1971 and released on his debut album in March 1972, this song reached number 8 on the Billboard Hot 100. Initially inspired by an eye infection, it evolved into a metaphor, as Browne described, for lost innocence and \"having seen too much.\" Graham Nash and David Crosby provided background vocals. Browne described hearing it on the radio for the first time as \"surreal.\" \"Dr. My Eyes was recorded in 1971 and released on Jackson Brown's debut album in March 1972 and it made it to number 8 on the Billboard Hot 100.\"","year":1972,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 41 - The Impact of The Troubadour on 1970s Popular Culture","url":"https://seventies.libsyn.com/ep-41-the-impact-of-the-troubadour-on-1970s-popular-culture"},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 41 - The Impact of The Troubadour on 1970s Popular Culture","url":null}]}],"book_tracks":[],"others":[{"id":159146835347323,"type":"album","name":"Jackson Browne","year":1972,"weight":18},{"id":204866009513996,"type":"album","name":"Late For The Sky","year":1974,"weight":16},{"id":3941390